          "distinct": true,
          "id": "a986bb28a21a901478ba44ff7c30649bf4bb220f",
          "message": "refactor(pixelflow-codegen): write the collapse loop once, for every ISA (#1060)\n\n## Four copies of one loop nest\n\nThe four `emit_collapse_loop` bodies were the same thing four times:\nallocate a frame, park X/Y/Z/W in slots above it, run the row loop\naround the batch loop, step X by a batch and Y by one, patch four\nbranches, tear down. Only the leaves differed. Four copies of control\nflow is four places for a branch fixup to drift.\n\nIt is now a **provided method** on `IsaBackend`, written once. What a\nbackend supplies is the meaning of each verb on its ISA:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `frame_alloc` / `frame_free` | `slot_store` / `slot_load` |\n| `counter_clear` / `counter_step` | `branch_if_counter_done` |\n| `store_result` / `advance_out` | `add_scalar` / `emit_ret` |\n\n## The defaults are where the interesting differences were\n\nFour verbs have defaults, and each names a difference that used to be an\nunstated one buried in a body:\n\n- **`body_frame_bytes`** is x86's red zone. The body spills *below*\n`rsp` and allocates nothing, so the scaffold's slots start at zero\nrather than above a frame that does not exist. Every other backend takes\nthe default.\n- **`scaffold_anchor` / `scaffold_finish`** are aarch64's constant pool\n— anchored inside the function after the frame exists, appended after\n`ret`.\n- **`latch_bounds`** is SysV's `mov r10, rcx`, needed only because the\nbody's gather clobbers `rcx`. AAPCS64 puts the bounds where the body\nnever reaches, so it does nothing.\n\n`Counter` and `OutStep` replace what used to be magic in the bodies.\n`Counter::{Batch,Row}` says *which* counter without naming `r9`/`x5`,\nand each backend maps it to its own register and its own bound in one\nplace:\n\n```rust\nconst fn counter_reg(counter: Counter) -> Gpr {\n    match counter { Counter::Batch => R9, Counter::Row => R11 }\n}\n```\n\nThe three x86 widths share that mapping outright — counters and the\noutput pointer are general-register ops, identical whether the body is\nSSE2, AVX2 or AVX-512 — so they live in one `x86_64::scaffold` module,\nthe same de-triplication argument as #1059's `sub rsp` / `add rsp` /\n`ret`.\n\n## Six arguments became one\n\n`emit_collapse_loop` took six arguments behind an\n`#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]`. They are one thing — the code\nthe scaffold wraps, produced by a single emit pass — so they are now one\n`CollapseBody`, and the allow is gone.\n\nThe `Result` went with it. Every verb is infallible, so `Ok(code)` was a\nwrapper over a value that could not fail.\n\n## Verified byte-identical\n\nThe emitted scaffold for **all four backends** is byte-for-byte\nunchanged, captured before and after across three frame shapes\n(red-zone, allocated, allocated + hoisted values) — twelve\nconfigurations, all identical.\n\nA permanent test replaces the scratch capture, pinning the property the\nshared branch displacements actually rest on: **the scaffold's size must\nnot depend on the frame it wraps.** A slot displacement that silently\nwidened from an 8-bit to a 32-bit form would move every label after it.\nIt needs no host CPU — emission is a pure function into `Vec<u8>`, so\nall four backends are measured from whichever machine runs the tests.\n\n## Dead code sweep\n\nStripping every dead-code suppression and building each ISA as `Native`\nin turn shows what is dead *in its own configuration*, rather than dead\nonly because another backend was selected.\n\n- **`Aarch64Asm`** — 420 lines wrapping 54 encoders in methods, with no\ncaller outside its own tests, which asserted it was byte-identical to\nthe free functions it delegated to. Its own doc said it \"adds nothing\nbut ergonomics and buffer ownership.\" The disassembler tests it hosted\nnow call the encoders the drivers actually use.\n- **`emit_prologue` / `emit_epilogue` / `emit_ternary`** in both\n`x86_64` and `aarch64` — pre-`IsaBackend` leftovers, zero callers.\n- `emit_cbnz_w16`, `emit_dup_s0`, `Pp::F2` (whose own doc read *\"no\nemitted op uses it yet\"*), and the AVX-512 `emit_or` / `emit_xor` /\n`emit_andn` / `emit_fmadd213` plus the two private encoders they\norphaned.\n- One `cfg_attr(..., allow(dead_code))` sitting on a `pub fn`, where it\nnever suppressed anything.\n\nZero dead-code warnings now remain at SSE2, AVX2, AVX-512 and aarch64,\nwith only the four intended per-driver allows in place.\n\n## Net\n\n−450 lines, and the loop nest, its branch fixups and its coordinate\nstepping exist exactly once.\n\n## Verification\n\n`cargo fmt --check`; clippy `--all-targets` clean on the workspace and\non `aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu`; tests at SSE2, AVX2+FMA and AVX-512F+DQ;\nworkspace green.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eji8JfyLCcTRmbbhyQCvWV)_\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
