          "id": "6336a0c2e6497bec7485d1c1720852735f7a04ba",
          "message": "feat(pixelflow-search): extraction prices a node by how often it runs (#1097)\n\n## Summary\n\nStage 1 of `docs/plans/2026-09-01-loop-aware-codegen.md`: **the cost of\na program is the cost of its execution, not of its text**, and the\nregion partition becomes a first-class thing rather than two hardcoded\ncalls.\n\n`extract_dag_scoped` multiplies each node's op cost by\n`LatticeShape::evals` of the variance of the form chosen for it — the\niterations of the innermost binder it depends on. With every extent\nknown at compile time (#1094) that is the exact instruction count of the\nunrolled program, not an ordinal preference. Per-class variance rides in\nthe same bottom-up DP as the cost, because the two determine each other.\n\n## What it buys, measured\n\nPinned deterministically by\n`scope_weighting_unfuses_an_fma_to_hoist_the_z_term`: one e-class\nholding `MulAdd(2, Z, X)` and `X + (Z + Z)`. Extraction picks the FMA\nwith no lattice — one instruction against two — and picks the **pair**\nover a 256×256 frame, because the fused form pays for `Z` at every\nsample while the split one lifts `Z + Z` out of the loop. The test\nbuilds both forms by hand, so it is a property of the cost model, not of\nhow far saturation got.\n\nThe plan's methodology note demands a measurement for any stage that\nchanges emitted code. On a full 1920×1080 collapse, best of seven:\n\n| hoistable terms | fused | scope-weighted | |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 1.522 ms | 1.499 ms | −1.5% |\n| 4 | 1.481 ms | 1.471 ms | −0.7% |\n| 8 | 1.767 ms | **1.568 ms** | **−11.3%** |\n\nThe win grows with what can leave the loop, as the model predicts. It is\nbought with accuracy, deliberately: un-fusing an FMA trades one rounding\nfor two per sample, so a glyph bake sits ~8.5e-6 from the interpreter\nwhere it used to sit ~1.1e-6. Range is untouched; precision is the\ntunable `CLAUDE.md` names.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`LatticeShape::evals(Variance)`** (`pixelflow-ir`) — the product of\nextents from the innermost depended-on axis outward. X-dependent runs\nper sample, Y-only per row, Z/W-only or constant once per call.\n- **`extract_dag_scoped`** carries `best_var` beside `best_cost`.\n`extract_dag` delegates with `POINT`, so every existing caller is\nunchanged.\n- **`ExtractionPolicy::for_lattice`** threads the shape;\n`optimize_runtime_arena` passes the one it already has. Default `POINT`\nweights everything by one — right for the `kernel!` macros, which expand\nbefore any bake.\n- **`CYCLE_COST`** rises from `1_000_000` to `usize::MAX / 4`: it was\nsafely above every *unweighted* cost, and a frame's weighting clears\nthat by orders of magnitude, which would have let a self-reference look\ncheaper than an expensive-but-legitimate form.\n- **`ScopedSchedule` / `partition_by_scope`** (`pixelflow-codegen`) —\nthe loop nest as data: one region per binder, outermost first, plus the\nper-sample body. It replaces two hardcoded `plan_collapse_hoist` calls\nwith one loop over binders, asking the same question at each level.\nEmitted code is **byte-identical** across a six-kernel corpus covering\nboth hoist tiers, an invariant root, and a transcendental.\n\n## The test that changed, and why\n\n`cached_glyph_matches_analytical_at_pixel_centers` compared a 32×32 bake\nagainst a point bake at 1e-5. That was bit-exact while every lattice\ncompiled identically; now a point and a frame are two compilations of\nthe same function, and the test landed either side of its tolerance\ndepending on saturation progress — 9.6e-6 locally, 2.5e-5 on a loaded\nrunner. It now compares against the **interpreter**, the reference\nimplementation rather than a sibling compilation, at 1e-4: ~12× headroom\nover the measured 8.5e-6, while a genuinely mistabulated glyph is off by\nO(0.1).\n\n## Noted, not acted on\n\nTrip-count weighting **multiplies cost-model error by the trip count**.\nThe FMA decision turns on the table pricing `MulAdd` at 5 cycles against\n`Add`'s 4 — a one-cycle difference that used to move one instruction and\nnow decides the shape of a whole loop body. `latency_prior` is a\n*latency* prior and a pixel loop is throughput-bound; that argues for\nmeasuring the table against loop bodies, which is its own piece of work.\n\n## Validation\n\n`cargo fmt --all --check`; `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D\nwarnings`; the aarch64 cross-clippy set; `cargo check -p pixelflow-ir\n--no-default-features`; test suites for ir / search / codegen / core /\ncompiler / graphics / pipeline — all clean. ISA matrix and the AVX-512 /\nAVX2 codegen runs are in flight on this head and will be reported before\nmerge.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Eji8JfyLCcTRmbbhyQCvWV\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
