          "distinct": true,
          "id": "3ced09bea40e8501db3c0164098fb47aa2a509d3",
          "message": "docs(designs): two designs for preempting a green actor mid-step\n\nThe mealy-transducer design rules preemption inside a handler out of scope\n(§1.3), on the reasoning that anything needing preemption gets its own\nthread or process. That holds until the long-running thing is a fused SIMD\nkernel — pixelflow's entire output — because the usual fix, cooperative\nsafe points in the inner loop, destroys exactly the codegen the compiler\nexists to produce.\n\nTwo alternatives, written up because the reasoning is worth keeping, not\nbecause either is being built. Neither is adopted; nothing has landed.\n\n1. Hardware-sandboxed preemption (KVM). Run the kernel in a guest, preempt\n   via timer signal, resume from the hardware-checkpointed vCPU. Unique\n   value is resumability — partial work survives — and async-signal-safety\n   becomes structural, since a guest cannot hold a host lock. Disqualified\n   as a scheduling primitive by the platform matrix: Linux-only, where the\n   runtime targets macOS and WASM too. Records the traps found in a\n   prototype that does not work as written (a 64-bit blob run in real mode,\n   and a lost-signal race in the same family as the doorbell bug).\n\n2. Preemption as transaction abort. Since a Mealy step keeps all surviving\n   state in `self`, an interrupted step needs nothing preserved: discard\n   the partial computation and retry. Preemption stops being a context\n   switch. The abort target already exists — `impl Transducer for Host`\n   makes the sweep loop the next Mealy machine up. Reduces to one\n   invariant (a step must not commit until it completes), which is the\n   same signature the design already wants for table-testability. Safety\n   rests on gating aborts by RIP range: the JIT knows where its kernels\n   live, and that region is exactly the code with no Drop locals, no\n   allocation, no locks. Records the livelock failure mode (a step longer\n   than the quantum never completes, silently) and the signal-mask trap\n   that would otherwise permit exactly one preemption.\n\nDesign 2's §8 notes the two pieces worth building regardless of any of\nthis — the pure-step signature and peek/commit on the inbox — and that the\nsignal machinery is gated on a step actually being observed to overrun,\nwhich has not happened yet.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PoZ14sG1PXMTr9MRd8g6gG",
          "timestamp": "2026-09-02T00:07:13-07:00",
          "tree_id": "a79fdfb9affbb95cc672a496c7e1ab897161bd1f",
          "url": "https://github.com/jppittman/pixelflow/commit/3ced09bea40e8501db3c0164098fb47aa2a509d3"
