          "distinct": true,
          "id": "3cec154451425e45940b91d56f52ce534422e4ac",
          "message": "feat(pixelflow-codegen): select_reload was a whole-kernel reservation for a per-instruction need (#1138)\n\n## Summary\n\nEscape-hatches step 2, the first of **class C**. The scratch a backend\ndeclares is now two named roles rather than one (`regalloc::Scratch {\ntemp, arm_reload }`), and `select_reload` has left `RegisterFile`.\n\nIt was the clearest case in the class: one register held out of *every*\nkernel's pool so that the rare kernel with a `Select` whose result and\nboth arms were all spilled had a third reload target — chosen at emit\ntime by a three-way case analysis over two fixed registers, justified by\na paragraph of positional reasoning. It is now a reservation the\nallocator makes for the length of one instruction, disjoint by\nconstruction from that instruction's operands, its destination and its\ntemp.\n\n## Pool effect\n\n| backend | pool before | pool after |\n|---|---|---|\n| SSE2 | 6 | **7** |\n| AVX2 | 5 | **6** |\n| AVX-512 | 23 | **24** |\n| aarch64 | 16 | 16 — see below |\n\n## Why it reserves for every Select\n\n`arm_reload` is reserved for every `Select`, not only the ones that turn\nout to need it, and that is forced rather than lazy: a value resident\nwhen its reader is allocated can still be evicted by a later\ninstruction, since `Placement` is one answer per value for its whole\nlife — so residency read at the point of reservation is not final.\n\nThat fixed point (which values spill ↔ how many reload registers each\ninstruction needs) is why the **rest of class C is a redesign of the\nallocator's output contract**, not another field, and why it isn't\nattempted here.\n\n## Measured, because over-reserving could have cost more than it buys\n\nOn the shape that maximizes the risk — *w* independent `Select` chains\nall live at once:\n\n| live selects | before (pool 6) | after (pool 7) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8 | 4 | 4 |\n| 12 | 9 | **8** |\n| 16 | 15 | **14** |\n| 24 | 27 | **26** |\n\nUnchanged or slightly better throughout: the register the pool gains\npays for the register a `Select` transiently borrows.\n\n## aarch64 gains nothing, and that is the finding\n\nv28 was `select_reload` **and** the register\n`emit_skip_if_all_false`/`_true` reduce a mask into with `UMAXV`/`UMINV`\n— two roles, one register, only one of them declared. Returning v28 to\nthe pool would have let the allocator hand it to a live value that a\n`Select`'s own short-circuit guard then clobbered, inside the collapse\nbody, on the one architecture this host cannot execute.\n\nIt is now `GUARD_SCRATCH` in `fixed`, where `RegisterFile::checked` can\nsee it. The x86 tiers need no equivalent: their guards go through\n`movmskps`/`kortest` and the flags, so they touch no vector register at\nall.\n\nThis is exactly what step 1's accounting exists to surface — an\nundeclared second role on a register whose first role was declared.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `cargo test --workspace` — 132 suites green\n- codegen suite additionally under `-C target-feature=+avx2,+fma` and\n`+avx512f,+avx512dq`\n- `cargo fmt --all --check`, workspace clippy, aarch64 cross-clippy\n\nNew tests: a `Select`'s `arm_reload` is a pool register disjoint from\nits operands, destination and temp; nothing but a `Select` reserves one.\nThe two tests that asserted `checked()` rejects a bad `select_reload`\nare gone with the field.\n\n## Not in this change\n\nThe rest of class C — `Placement::Spilled` and `reload` — for the reason\ngiven above.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Eji8JfyLCcTRmbbhyQCvWV\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude\nCode](https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eji8JfyLCcTRmbbhyQCvWV)_\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
